# First, check if a key pair is already generated in the WSL environment:
>ls -al ~/.ssh/id_*.pub

# 2.Generate a new SSH key:
> ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "your_email@example.com"

# Start the SSH agent and add your key:
> eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"
> ssh-add ~/.ssh/id_ed25519

Verification: The terminal should output Identity added: /home/mando/.ssh/id_ed25519...

# Output your public key to the terminal and copy the entire string (it will start with ssh-ed25519):
> cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub

# Add the public key to GitHub: Navigate to GitHub Settings > SSH and GPG keys > New SSH key, give it a recognizable name

# Test the connection to verify that GitHub now recognizes your machine:
> ssh -T git@github.com
Verification: You should see a success message saying Hi Cephamando! You've successfully authenticated...


# If pushing unversioned folder from local pc to a brand-new Git repository.
> git init
 
# else Clone the repo:
> git clone git@github.com:your_github_username/your_target_repo.git


#If needed: disconnect local repository from the remote repository currently named "origin" to link the local code to a brand new repository created and/or on another GitHub account or 
> git remote remove origin

# Set the base fetch URL to GitHub (using SSH)
> git remote add origin git@github.com:your-username/your-git-repo.git

# Add GitHub as a push destination
> git remote set-url --add --push origin git@github.com:your-username/your-git-repo.git

# Add GitLab as a push destination
> git remote set-url --add --push origin git@gitlab.com:your-username/your-git-repo.git

